Peak positions separate cleanly into three coherent families: wolframite MnWO₄ is dominant; a rhombohedral Mn₃WO₆ family explains otherwise unassigned lines near 19.7, 20.2, 28.5, 31.7, 35.0, and 46.4°; and residual monoclinic WO₃ explains the 22.9/24.2° and 33–34° multiplets. I’m testing the competing WO₃ settings and phase inventories by identical staged Rietveld refinements, rather than relying on peak coincidence alone.

The three-phase inventory is quantitatively self-consistent: the provisional fractions are about 62% MnWO₄, 18% Mn₃WO₆, and 20% WO₃, which reconstructs the stated 1:1 Mn:W precursor ratio. The monoclinic WO₃ model clearly outperforms tetragonal, orthorhombic, and alternative monoclinic cells. I also found an important modeling detail in the supplied generator: reference CIFs were symmetry-normalized and assigned common isotropic displacement factors before synthesis, so I’m rebuilding the starting models in that same representation before refining coordinates and texture.

With texture, anisotropic broadening, cell metrics, and surface effects included, the fit is now visually close and the weighted profile residual is 2.85%. The remaining discrepancy is concentrated in subtle intensity/position differences at strong reflections. I’m doing one final structure-sensitive pass from the unperturbed registered coordinates, with physically bounded displacements, so that better profile agreement does not come at the cost of implausible oxygen geometry.

